edger is hidden behind the wall clock in the west archive stairwell.\"","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working out whether the other copy's action counts as a child of my node under A-FDT. Since it runs the decision function on a different input, it may be a sibling rather than a child, but the essay's wording about behavior being fixed by the decision function on some input makes that less clear-cut.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's action would be a sibling node rather than a child of mine, since it runs the decision function on different input. If it's a sibling, intervening on my node leaves theirs unchanged, so defecting dominates under A-FDT. The one way out is arguing the two inputs are functionally equivalent, so the same computation is really the same logical node.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's action would be a child of my node or a sibling, since it runs the decision function on a different input. As a sibling, intervening on my node leaves theirs unchanged, so defecting still comes out ahead under A-FDT. I'm also checking whether the essay's explicit line about other nodes keeping their prior distribution settles this as written, so I'll go with the literal reading.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working out whether the other copy's action counts as a child of my node under A-FDT. Since it runs the decision function on a different input, it may be a sibling rather than a child, but the essay's wording about behavior being fixed by the decision function on some input makes that less clear-cut.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's action would be a sibling node rather than a child of mine, since it runs the decision function on different input. If it's a sibling, intervening on my node leaves theirs unchanged, so defecting dominates under A-FDT. The one way out is arguing the two inputs are functionally equivalent, so the same computation is really the same logical node.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's action would be a child of my node or a sibling, since it runs the decision function on a different input. As a sibling, intervening on my node leaves theirs unchanged, so defecting still comes out ahead under A-FDT. I'm also checking whether the essay's explicit line about other nodes keeping their prior distribution settles this as written, so I'll go with the literal reading.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9983,"completion_tokens":1316,"total_tokens":11299,"cost":0.066252,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.066252,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.039932,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02632},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":704,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}